 r←{x}Initial_NuGet ps;netcore;msg
 ∆DotNetIsAvailable←netcore←IsDotNetAvailable
⍝ Whether to ask at all is decided by `netcore`: without .NET the tests cannot be executed whatever
⍝ the answer would be. Asking first and declaring them impossible afterwards, which is what this used
⍝ to do, just wastes the user's time.
 :If 0=netcore
     ∆ExecNuGetTest←0
 :ElseIf ~ps.batchFlag
 :AndIf ~∨/' cider_fns="Test_NuGet_'⍷#.GetCommandLine
     ∆ExecNuGetTest←CommTools.YesOrNo'Do you want to execute NuGet-related test cases?'
 :Else
     ∆ExecNuGetTest←1
 :EndIf
 msg←''
 :If 0=netcore
     msg←'.NET is not available.',(⎕UCS 13),'Therefore the NuGet tests cannot be executed.'
 :ElseIf ∆ExecNuGetTest
     :Trap 0
         :If 0=≢⎕SH'dotnet --list-sdks'
             msg←'It appears that no .NET SDK is installed, therefore the NuGet tests cannot be executed'
         :EndIf
     :Else
         msg←'Executing:  dotnet --info  caused an error, therefore the NuGet tests cannot be executed'
     :EndTrap
 :EndIf
⍝ A message means the tests cannot run, in batch mode just as much as interactively. The result used to
⍝ be overwritten by the last line, so in batch a missing .NET was reported as success and the group was
⍝ executed all the same.
 :If 0<≢msg
     ∆ExecNuGetTest←0
     :If ~ps.batchFlag
         CommTools.Pause msg
     :EndIf
 :EndIf
 r←∆ExecNuGetTest
⍝Done
